---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Memory map"
  description="What lives at each address range in the DS's main memory while the game is running."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'How the Game Works', href: `${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/` },
    { label: 'Memory map' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The DS gives the game 4 MB of main memory, from
    <code>0x02000000</code> to <code>0x02400000</code>. Here's roughly
    what's where while the game is playing.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Address range</th><th>What's there</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0x02000000 – 0x020A87A0</code></td><td>ARM9 binary (the always-loaded core). About 700 KB of code and data after the boot decompression.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x020A87A0 – 0x02125420</code></td><td>ARM9 zero-initialized memory (BSS). Game state, content cache tables, sprite-slot arrays.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x02125420 – 0x02155A00</code></td><td>Overlay BSS. Per-overlay uninitialized data.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x02155A00 – 0x021570A0</code></td><td>Shared overlay slot 1 — game mode controllers (66 candidates).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021597A0 – 0x021643E0</code></td><td>Shared overlay slot 2 — screen body renderers (25 candidates).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x0215C540 – 0x0215DE40</code></td><td>Shared overlay slot 3 — game-mode logic (20 candidates).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x0216DA40 – 0x0218E2A0</code></td><td>The rendering engine. ov137 lives here most of the time, swapped with ov138 when 3D mode is active.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021B6080 – 0x021C3420</code></td><td>Shared overlay slot 4 (ov156 / ov157).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021C3420 – 0x021FB320</code></td><td>Shared overlay slot 5 (ov158 / ov159).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021FB3C0 – 0x02201000</code></td><td>Shared overlay slot 6.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x023FFE00 – 0x023FFFFC</code></td><td>Stack, per the DS spec.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The game state struct</h2>
  <p>
    The most-referenced address in the game is <code>0x021A55C0</code>,
    with about 750 references. It's a pointer to the game state struct
    — a big record holding the player's current screen, their inventory
    state, the current dialog cursor, and so on. Every overlay's init
    function typically picks this up first and reads fields out of it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The struct's fields aren't fully labeled, but we can see common
    access patterns. For example, lots of functions read
    <code>state-&gt;field_at_0x4C</code> to get a sub-record. We don't
    always know what those sub-records hold without tracing further.
  </p>

  <h2>The system clock</h2>
  <p>
    The game reads the DS's real-world clock and uses the time of day
    to pick which title screen variant to show (day castle vs night
    castle), to decide which shops are open, and to drive several
    other in-game systems. There's no in-game time-skip — the only way
    to advance time is to actually wait, or to change the DS's clock.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the one feature of the game that genuinely depends on
    hardware state outside the cart, and it affects what an emulator
    user sees too: setting the emulator's clock to a different time
    will get you a different title screen variant.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
